{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790290008,"nonce":"VSUsJLRezjUhPc2T2Ooc5vUzOlE=","title":"Python: zypy runs the program on the main thread, whose stack differs by platform","message":"`zypy` compiled and ran the program on the process's main thread: 8 MB on Linux and macOS, 1 MB on Windows. The debug build spends enough stack per Python call that `def f(n): return 0 if n == 0 else 1 + f(n - 1)` overflows at f(300) with 8 MB and at f(100) with 1 MB, well inside CPython's default recursion limit of 1000.\n\nReproduced on macOS: running the Python conformance suite with zypy's main thread limited to 1 MB (`ulimit -s 1024`, which the spawned zypy inherits) fails 15 of 25 categories (35 cases) with stack overflows; at 8 MB they all pass. The Windows CI job would hit exactly this once it reaches the suite.\n\nFix: zypy runs the program on a spawned 1 GB thread, as zylua already does, and propagates a panic. With it the suite passes with the main thread at 1 MB and f(900) runs.","files":null}]}